M3.2: physical frame allocator (E820 -> LIFO frame stack)

- pmm.c: type-1 runs from the E820 boot ABI pushed ascending into a
  free-frame stack (32768-frame table in .bss = 128 MiB capacity).
  Reserved-out: everything below 1 MiB (BIOS estate: stage 1/2, page
  tables still referenced by CR3) and everything through the kernel's
  page-rounded __bss_end, so neither boot nor kernel data can ever
  collide with an allocated frame. Reclaiming stage-2 territory waits
  for the kernel to own its own page tables.
- API: pmm_alloc/pmm_free/pmm_free_frames; pmm_free refuses
  unaligned/below-limit frames and O(n)-scans against double free
  (honest, deliberate - the O(1) bitmap arrives with the M3.4
  heap/bitmap slice; MLFQ-style allocator games deferred).
- mem.c refactored into a reusable iterator (memmap_count/get) so
  pmm_init reads the same ABI source of truth instead of a copy.
- main.c self-test: 3 consecutive allocs (aligned, distinct,
  descending from the run top), one deliberate double-free that must
  be refused, then balance restored.

Evidence (QEMU 128 MiB): frames=0x7e8c (~126.5 MiB above the kernel,
matches the map arithmetic), allocs 0x7fdf000/0x7fde000/0x7fdd000,
"pmm: double free 0x7fdf000" refusal line, "pmm: selftest ok", and
the M2.4 scheduler demo green after allocator bring-up.
